{"additions":27,"author":{"id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIyMDg1Mzg3","is_bot":false,"login":"itsmaleen","name":"Marlin Jayasekera"},"baseRefName":"main","body":"## What\n\nHijacking the hardware volume buttons is surprising as a default — the buttons stop changing volume the moment the workspace view appears. This puts the behavior behind a `volumeButtonControls` preference that **defaults to off**.\n\n## Changes\n\n- **`cmuxApp.swift`** — register `volumeButtonControls: false` alongside the existing `autoSubmitSpeech` default.\n- **`WorkspaceLayoutView.swift`** — gate both `volumeHandler.start()` sites (initial `onAppear` and the `didBecomeActive` restart) on the flag, plus an `onChange(of:)` that starts or `stop()`s the handler so flipping the toggle takes effect immediately without a relaunch.\n- **`SettingsView.swift`** — new \"Walkie-Talkie\" section with the toggle and a footnote describing the four gestures (up = start/send dictation, double-up = quick actions, down = cycle surfaces, double-down = cycle workspaces).\n\n## Behavior with the flag off\n\nNothing is attached: no `MPVolumeView` in the window hierarchy, no `AVAudioSession` category change, no `outputVolume` KVO observer. The buttons behave as ordinary system volume controls.\n\nThe touch equivalents are untouched, so the app is fully usable with the feature disabled — long-press for quick actions, horizontal swipe to cycle surfaces, and the compose bar.\n\n## Testing\n\n`xcodebuild -target cmux -sdk iphonesimulator26.5` — build succeeded.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)","changedFiles":3,"deletions":3,"headRefName":"feat/volume-button-toggle","labels":[],"state":"OPEN","title":"feat(ios): make volume-button walkie-talkie controls opt-in"}
